#     grep -rn --include='*.py' _is_text_read src tests | wc -l
# and printed 0. Afterwards casting 3's e1693db widened the pin and wrote the
# history comment naming the three orphans the widening catches --
# `tests/test_spawn_progress.py#_is_text_read` among them, at
# tests/test_orchestrator_gates.py:3659. The grep reads that comment as a live
# reference, so the same command now prints 1 and the log stopped reproducing.
#
# Nothing about the fix went stale: the two orphans are still deleted and the
# widened pin still passes. A comment citing a deleted helper is history, not a
# reference. D-196 recorded this exact trap about its own pin -- its first draft
# was a text sweep and PASSED at the pre-fix commit, because the comment
# explaining the pin spelled the name -- and casting 1 hit it on the same day
# in evidence/casting-1-grind-cycle-17.log. What was wrong was the MEASUREMENT,
# so the measurement is what changed here and nothing else did.
#
# The sweep below parses every .py file under src/ and tests/ and asks whether
# each name appears as an ast.Name, an ast.Attribute or an exact-string
# ast.Constant -- `_named_in_code` from tests/test_orchestrator_gates.py, the
# pin's own rule. A comment is invisible to it, and so is a docstring that
# merely MENTIONS the name, because the constant arm is exact-match and not
# substring. Both orphans are deleted, so neither has a def of its own to
# exclude: any reappearance at all, definition or reference, is a non-zero
# count here, which is the direction this check should err in.
#
# ALL FOUR CHECKS MOVED, not only the two that had to. `_decode_site still
# defined` was `grep -c '^def _decode_site('`, which a `#` comment cannot match
# and which was therefore already comment-blind; `_TEXT_READ_RAISES still
